**Dual-write.** Legacy `pg-orders-1` still serves every read and write. Each write also adds a row to `orders_outbox` in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` replays those rows to orders-v2 asynchronously. `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night. Nothing reads from orders-v2 yet, so customers can't see any errors there. The phase passes when four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the `order-diff` mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for the same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum comparison matches.

**Cutover.** The flag `orders.read_from_v2` moves reads to orders-v2 at 1%, 10%, 50% and 100% of requests, one step per day. Writes still go to both stores during the ramp, so rolling back only means setting that flag to 0. After a 48-hour soak at 100%, `orders.write_legacy` is turned off. That step is one-way: nothing syncs orders-v2 back to legacy, so it needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. Legacy then stays up read-only for 30 days before it is decommissioned.

The schedule has no slack. If the write stop misses 2026-11-30, it waits until January because of the code freeze, and dual-write keeps running until then.

